"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[75033],{775033:e=>{e.exports="# Scripting & Control Flow\n\nSource dialect procedural control (IF/ELSIF, LOOP, WHILE, FOR, dynamic SQL, error handling) → DBSQL/PySpark equivalents.\n\n> For DBSQL scripting syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/sql-scripting.md`.\n> For DBSQL dynamic SQL, load `skills/writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n> **Scripting context vs. stored procedure context**: All `DECLARE` examples in this file show standalone notebook scripting syntax, where `DECLARE` appears at the top level outside any `BEGIN...END`. When converting an Oracle PL/SQL procedure or anonymous block, these same patterns must be placed **inside** the procedure's `BEGIN...END` block, before any executable statements (and `DECLARE HANDLER` declarations must come before any non-declarative statement). Placing `DECLARE` before the procedure's `AS BEGIN` causes `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR`. See `04-procedures-variables.md` for the correct in-procedure structure.\n\n## ORACLE\n\n### Control flow\n\n| Oracle (PL/SQL)                                           | DBSQL                                       | Notes                                                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `IF x = 1 THEN ... END IF;`                               | `IF x = 1 THEN ... END IF;`                 | Same shape — just confirm no PL/SQL-only syntax remains                                             |\n| `ELSIF x = 2 THEN ...`                                    | `ELSEIF x = 2 THEN ...`                     | **Spelling change**: `ELSIF` → `ELSEIF`                                                             |\n| `CASE x WHEN 1 THEN ... ELSE ... END CASE;` (statement)   | `CASE x WHEN 1 THEN ... ELSE ... END CASE;` | DBSQL CASE-statement syntax matches; see CASE subsection for `END CASE` and missing-`ELSE` drift    |\n| `CASE WHEN cond THEN ... END CASE;` (searched, statement) | `CASE WHEN cond THEN ... END CASE;`         | Same shape; DBSQL also supports `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... END` as an **expression** in `SET`/`SELECT` |\n| `LOOP ... EXIT WHEN cond; END LOOP;`                      | `WHILE NOT (cond) DO ... END WHILE;`        | DBSQL has no bare `LOOP`; invert the exit condition into `WHILE`                                    |\n| `WHILE i < 10 LOOP ... END LOOP;`                         | `WHILE i < 10 DO ... END WHILE;`            | `LOOP/END LOOP` → `DO/END WHILE`                                                                    |\n| `FOR i IN 1..10 LOOP ... END LOOP;`                       | `FOR i IN 1..10 DO ... END FOR;`            | DBSQL `FOR` requires labelled `END FOR`; verify DBR support                                         |\n| `FOR rec IN (SELECT ...) LOOP ...`                        | `FOR rec AS SELECT ... DO ... END FOR;`     | Implicit cursor FOR loop → DBSQL `FOR ... AS`                                                       |\n| `EXIT;` / `EXIT WHEN cond;`                               | `LEAVE loop_label;`                         | Label required in DBSQL; introduce a label on the loop                                              |\n| `CONTINUE;` / `CONTINUE WHEN cond;`                       | `ITERATE loop_label;`                       | Label required                                                                                      |\n| `GOTO label; ... <<label>>`                               | Refactor — no GOTO in DBSQL                 | Rewrite as labelled WHILE + conditional `LEAVE`/`ITERATE`                                           |\n| `NULL;` (no-op)                                           | Remove or replace with `BEGIN END;`         | DBSQL has no statement-level `NULL;` no-op                                                          |\n| `DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('msg')`                             | `SELECT 'msg';` or notebook log             | See `04-procedures-variables.md → DBMS_OUTPUT`                                                      |\n| `RETURN;` / `RETURN value;`                               | `RETURN;` / `RETURN value;`                 | Same for procedures and functions                                                                   |\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle (PL/SQL)\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'active';\nELSIF v_count = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nELSE\n    NULL;\nEND IF;\n\n-- DBSQL\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.t SET status = 'active';\nELSEIF v_count = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nEND IF;\n-- Oracle's bare `NULL;` no-op was dropped — DBSQL has no equivalent and an empty branch is implicit.\n```\n\n### `CASE` statement vs. `CASE` expression\n\nOracle has both forms; DBSQL supports both, but with two semantic gotchas worth flagging during migration.\n\n**Form 1 — searched CASE statement** (procedural, ends with `END CASE`):\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nBEGIN\n    CASE\n        WHEN p_amount > 1000 THEN UPDATE orders SET tier = 'gold'  WHERE id = p_id;\n        WHEN p_amount > 100  THEN UPDATE orders SET tier = 'silver' WHERE id = p_id;\n        ELSE                       UPDATE orders SET tier = 'bronze' WHERE id = p_id;\n    END CASE;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — same shape\nBEGIN\n    CASE\n        WHEN p_amount > 1000 THEN UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET tier = 'gold'   WHERE id = p_id;\n        WHEN p_amount > 100  THEN UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET tier = 'silver' WHERE id = p_id;\n        ELSE                       UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET tier = 'bronze' WHERE id = p_id;\n    END CASE;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Form 2 — simple CASE statement** (`CASE selector WHEN value THEN ...`):\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nCASE p_status\n    WHEN 'A' THEN UPDATE orders SET active = 1 WHERE id = p_id;\n    WHEN 'I' THEN UPDATE orders SET active = 0 WHERE id = p_id;\nEND CASE;\n\n-- DBSQL — same shape (still ends with END CASE for the statement form)\n```\n\n**Form 3 — CASE expression** (returns a value, no `CASE` keyword needed in front of the assignment, ends with `END` not `END CASE`):\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nv_tier := CASE WHEN p_amount > 1000 THEN 'gold' ELSE 'bronze' END;\n\n-- DBSQL\nSET v_tier = CASE WHEN p_amount > 1000 THEN 'gold' ELSE 'bronze' END;\n```\n\n**Drift to flag:**\n\n- **`CASE_NOT_FOUND` exception.** Oracle raises `CASE_NOT_FOUND` (ORA-06592) when the CASE **statement** has no matching `WHEN` and no `ELSE`. DBSQL silently does nothing. If the source explicitly relied on `CASE_NOT_FOUND` (e.g. caught in an outer handler), add an explicit `ELSE` branch that `SIGNAL`s the error so the behavior is preserved. CASE _expressions_ return `NULL` on no-match in both dialects — no drift.\n- **`END CASE` vs `END`.** Statement form ends with `END CASE;`; expression form ends with `END`. A common conversion mistake is to drop `CASE` from `END CASE` when copy-pasting — DBSQL parsing tolerates both in many contexts but will reject `END CASE` inside an expression position.\n\n### EXISTS / row-presence patterns — three distinct cases\n\nOracle has no `IF EXISTS(...)` statement. The same intent is expressed via `SELECT COUNT(*)`, `SELECT ... INTO` with `NO_DATA_FOUND`, or queries against the data dictionary. Each shape translates differently.\n\n**Case 1 — Row-presence check via `COUNT(*)`**:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle (row-presence: does the periods table have any rows?)\nDECLARE\n    v_count NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_count FROM periods;\n    IF v_count > 0 THEN\n        do_something();\n    END IF;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL (standalone notebook scripting; inside a procedure, this DECLARE goes inside BEGIN...END)\nDECLARE v_count INT DEFAULT 0;\nSET v_count = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.periods);\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.do_something();\nEND IF;\n```\n\nUse the variable form as the primary translation. Avoid `IF (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t) > 0` directly in the IF condition — subquery-in-IF validity inside DBSQL procedure bodies is not universally confirmed.\n\n**Case 2 — Row-presence check via `SELECT ... INTO` + `NO_DATA_FOUND`**:\n\nThis Oracle idiom uses an exception block as control flow — a row was found vs. not found. Translate to a `COUNT(*)` variable check, not to a `DECLARE HANDLER`.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    v_id NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT id INTO v_id FROM customers WHERE email = p_email;\n    -- found: do something with v_id\n    update_customer(v_id);\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN\n        -- not found: do something else\n        insert_customer(p_email);\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — convert exception-as-control-flow into an explicit existence check\nDECLARE v_id BIGINT;\nSET v_id = (SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.customers WHERE email = p_email);\nIF v_id IS NOT NULL THEN\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.update_customer(v_id);\nELSE\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.insert_customer(p_email);\nEND IF;\n```\n\nDo not translate `WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND` to `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND` — that would exit the block, dropping the `ELSE` branch logic.\n\n**Case 3 — Object-existence check** (queries against `USER_*`, `ALL_*`, `DBA_*` data dictionary views):\n\nTranslate to the DBSQL DDL form, not a row-count check.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle (object-existence: does procedure SP_ABS exist?)\nDECLARE\n    v_count NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_count\n    FROM user_objects\n    WHERE object_name = 'SP_ABS' AND object_type = 'PROCEDURE';\n    IF v_count > 0 THEN\n        EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'DROP PROCEDURE sp_abs';\n    END IF;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nDROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS my_catalog.my_schema.sp_abs;\n\n-- Oracle (table existence — Global Temporary Table)\nDECLARE\n    v_count NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_count FROM user_tables WHERE table_name = 'STAGING';\n    IF v_count > 0 THEN\n        EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'DROP TABLE staging';\n    END IF;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.my_schema.staging;\n```\n\nDistinguishing the patterns: if the source query reads from `USER_OBJECTS`, `ALL_OBJECTS`, `DBA_OBJECTS`, `USER_TABLES`, `ALL_TABLES`, `USER_PROCEDURES`, `USER_VIEWS`, `USER_SEQUENCES`, `USER_INDEXES`, etc., it is Case 3 (object-existence) — use `DROP ... IF EXISTS` / `CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS`. If the query reads from a user data table without these dictionary references, it is Case 1 or Case 2 (row-presence).\n\n### Bare `LOOP ... EXIT WHEN ... END LOOP`\n\nDBSQL has no unconditional infinite loop. Invert the `EXIT WHEN` condition into a `WHILE`:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    i NUMBER := 0;\nBEGIN\n    LOOP\n        INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\n        i := i + 1;\n        EXIT WHEN i >= 10;\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 0;\nWHILE i < 10 DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n    SET i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\nIf the loop has multiple `EXIT WHEN` checkpoints scattered through the body, introduce a labelled outer loop and `LEAVE`:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL\nloop_label: WHILE TRUE DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n    SET i = i + 1;\n    IF i >= 10 THEN LEAVE loop_label; END IF;\n    -- ... more body ...\n    IF some_other_condition THEN LEAVE loop_label; END IF;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\n### Numeric `FOR` loop\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nBEGIN\n    FOR i IN 1..10 LOOP\n        INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nFOR i IN 1..10 DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\nEND FOR;\n-- If FOR ... IN range is not supported in your DBR version, fall back to WHILE:\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 1;\nWHILE i <= 10 DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n    SET i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\nOracle's `REVERSE` keyword (`FOR i IN REVERSE 1..10`) has no direct DBSQL equivalent — rewrite as a decrementing `WHILE`.\n\n### Cursor `FOR` loop (implicit cursor)\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nBEGIN\n    FOR rec IN (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE active = 1) LOOP\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(rec.id || ': ' || rec.name);\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — preferred: rewrite as set-based SQL\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit_log\nSELECT CONCAT(id, ': ', name) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees WHERE active = 1;\n\n-- DBSQL — literal translation if procedural iteration is required\nFOR rec AS SELECT id, name FROM my_catalog.my_schema.employees WHERE active = 1 DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit_log VALUES (CONCAT(rec.id, ': ', rec.name));\nEND FOR;\n```\n\nFor literal cursor translation (`OPEN/FETCH/CLOSE` and `BULK COLLECT`), see `04a-cursors.md`.\n\n### Error handling\n\nOracle's `EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN` block sits at the end of a `BEGIN ... END;` block. DBSQL uses `DECLARE ... HANDLER` declared **before** any executable statements in the block.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1);\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No data');\n    WHEN OTHERS THEN\n        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Error: ' || SQLERRM);\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE error_msg STRING;\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 error_msg = MESSAGE_TEXT;\n        SELECT error_msg;\n    END;\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (1);\nEND;\n```\n\nKey shape changes:\n\n- **Position**: handler moves from end of block (Oracle `EXCEPTION` clause) to top of block (DBSQL `DECLARE HANDLER`).\n- **Semantics**: `EXIT HANDLER` exits the current `BEGIN ... END` (matches Oracle `EXCEPTION` exit semantics). `CONTINUE HANDLER` resumes after the failing statement.\n- **Per-exception branches**: Oracle's multiple `WHEN` clauses (`NO_DATA_FOUND`, `TOO_MANY_ROWS`, named exceptions) collapse into one `SQLEXCEPTION` handler in DBSQL — discriminate inside the handler body using `GET DIAGNOSTICS` if needed, or split into nested blocks.\n- See `04b-error-handling.md` for the named-exception mapping table and re-raise semantics (`RAISE` → `RESIGNAL`).\n\n### `RAISE` / `RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR` → SIGNAL\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nRAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'Custom error');\nRAISE my_named_exception;\nRAISE; -- re-raise inside a handler\n\n-- DBSQL\nSIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Custom error';\nSIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'my_named_exception triggered';\nRESIGNAL;\n```\n\nOracle's error code `-20001` (and the `-20000`..`-20999` user range) has no direct SQLSTATE representation — embed the original code in the message text if downstream callers parse it.\n\n### Implicit cursor attributes (`SQL%...`)\n\nOracle exposes the most-recent DML's outcome through implicit cursor attributes. DBSQL uses `GET DIAGNOSTICS`.\n\n| Oracle         | DBSQL                                                          | Notes                                                     |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SQL%ROWCOUNT` | `GET DIAGNOSTICS row_count = ROW_COUNT;`                       | Read into a `DECLARE`d variable immediately after the DML |\n| `SQL%FOUND`    | `IF row_count > 0 THEN ...` (after capturing `ROW_COUNT`)      | No direct boolean; derive from `ROW_COUNT`                |\n| `SQL%NOTFOUND` | `IF row_count = 0 THEN ...`                                    | No direct boolean; derive from `ROW_COUNT`                |\n| `SQL%ISOPEN`   | Always `FALSE` for implicit cursors in Oracle — drop the check | Has no meaningful translation                             |\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nUPDATE orders SET status = 'done' WHERE id = 42;\nIF SQL%ROWCOUNT = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO orders (id, status) VALUES (42, 'done');\nEND IF;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE row_count INT DEFAULT 0;\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET status = 'done' WHERE id = 42;\nGET DIAGNOSTICS row_count = ROW_COUNT;\nIF row_count = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (id, status) VALUES (42, 'done');\nEND IF;\n```\n\n`GET DIAGNOSTICS` reads the most recently executed DML — capture the value into a variable immediately after the statement, before any other DML overwrites it.\n\n> ⚠️ **Common mistake — `ROW_COUNT()` as a function call is not the DBSQL idiom.** Other dialects (notably MySQL) expose the affected-row count as a built-in function `ROW_COUNT()`. **DBSQL does not.** Inside a `LANGUAGE SQL` procedure body, the only correct form is the diagnostics statement above:\n>\n> ```sql\n> -- WRONG — ROW_COUNT() function call, will not parse / return the wrong value\n> IF ROW_COUNT() = 0 THEN ...\n>\n> -- RIGHT — capture into a DECLAREd variable via GET DIAGNOSTICS\n> DECLARE row_count INT DEFAULT 0;\n> UPDATE ...;\n> GET DIAGNOSTICS row_count = ROW_COUNT;\n> IF row_count = 0 THEN ...\n> ```\n>\n> The `ROW_COUNT` token on the right-hand side of `GET DIAGNOSTICS` is a diagnostic-statement keyword, not a callable function — note the absence of parentheses. This trips up converters that auto-translate `SQL%ROWCOUNT` by treating it as a \"function-like\" Oracle construct; it isn't.\n\n### Dynamic SQL\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    v_sql VARCHAR2(4000);\nBEGIN\n    v_sql := 'SELECT * FROM ' || p_table_name;\n    EXECUTE IMMEDIATE v_sql;\n    -- with bind variables (Oracle uses :1, :2 or :name)\n    EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'UPDATE orders SET status = :1 WHERE id = :2'\n        USING 'done', 42;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL (standalone notebook scripting; inside a procedure, DECLARE goes inside BEGIN...END)\nDECLARE sql_stmt STRING DEFAULT CONCAT('SELECT * FROM ', table_name);\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_stmt;\n\n-- With runtime parameter binding: ? placeholders, resolved positionally via USING.\n-- Oracle :1, :2 (positional) and :name (named) bind variables both become positional ?\n-- in DBSQL. The order of arguments in USING must match the order of ? placeholders.\nDECLARE sql_with_param STRING\n    DEFAULT 'UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET status = ? WHERE id = ?';\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_with_param USING 'done', 42;\n```\n\nIdentifier injection (Oracle concatenates table/column names directly into the SQL string) should use `IDENTIFIER()` in DBSQL where the dynamic part is an object name rather than a value. See `04-procedures-variables.md → EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` for the parameter syntax matrix.\n\n> For full `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` and `IDENTIFIER()` syntax, load `writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n### `GOTO` and labels\n\nOracle PL/SQL supports `GOTO label;` and `<<label>>` markers. DBSQL has no `GOTO`. Refactor into structured control flow:\n\n- Forward jump skipping a block → wrap the skipped block in `IF NOT condition THEN ... END IF;`.\n- Jump to end of loop → labelled `LEAVE loop_label;`.\n- Jump to next iteration → labelled `ITERATE loop_label;`.\n- Jump backward (re-entry) → wrap the target region in a `WHILE TRUE DO ... END WHILE;` with an `IF ... LEAVE` exit condition.\n\nIf the source `GOTO` graph is not reducible (multiple entry points, crossed jumps), mark it:\n\n```sql\n-- TODO: manual review required — non-reducible GOTO graph; rewrite control flow by hand.\n```\n\n**Loop labels (`<<outer>>`).** Oracle places labels in `<<...>>` markers above the loop and references them in `EXIT outer;` / `CONTINUE outer;`. DBSQL uses prefix labels (`outer:`) with `LEAVE outer;` / `ITERATE outer;`:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\n<<outer>>\nFOR i IN 1..10 LOOP\n    <<inner>>\n    FOR j IN 1..10 LOOP\n        IF some_cond THEN EXIT outer; END IF;\n        IF other_cond THEN CONTINUE outer; END IF;\n    END LOOP inner;\nEND LOOP outer;\n\n-- DBSQL\nouter: WHILE i <= 10 DO\n    inner: WHILE j <= 10 DO\n        IF some_cond THEN LEAVE outer; END IF;\n        IF other_cond THEN ITERATE outer; END IF;\n        SET j = j + 1;\n    END WHILE inner;\n    SET i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE outer;\n```\n\nThe trailing label after `END LOOP outer` / `END WHILE outer` is optional in both dialects but useful for nested-loop readability — keep it during migration.\n\n### Conditional compilation (`$IF` / `$THEN` / `$ELSIF` / `$ELSE` / `$END` / `$ERROR`)\n\nOracle PL/SQL has a **preprocessor** that selects code at compile time based on `$$identifier` flags, the `PLSQL_CCFLAGS` session parameter, and `DBMS_DB_VERSION` constants:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle: pick a branch at compile time based on the database version\nBEGIN\n    $IF DBMS_DB_VERSION.VERSION >= 19 $THEN\n        -- 19c+ optimised path\n        UPDATE orders SET status = 'done' WHERE id = p_id;\n    $ELSE\n        -- legacy fallback\n        my_legacy_update_proc(p_id);\n    $END\n\n    -- Or driven by a deployment flag set via PLSQL_CCFLAGS = 'debug:true'\n    $IF $$debug $THEN DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('p_id=' || p_id); $END\nEND;\n/\n```\n\n**DBSQL has no preprocessor.** Translation strategy depends on what the conditional gates:\n\n- **Version-based branch (`DBMS_DB_VERSION.VERSION`)** — pick the branch that matched the source Oracle version that was migrated from, drop the directives entirely, and discard the dead branch. The migrated code targets a single Databricks runtime; there is no compile-time version dispatch.\n- **Deployment-flag branch (`$$debug`, `$$trace`, etc.)** — replace with a runtime `IF` against a session-scoped variable or a configuration row in a small Delta table:\n\n  ```sql\n  -- DBSQL — runtime equivalent of $IF $$debug $THEN ... $END\n  DECLARE debug_enabled BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE;\n  SET debug_enabled = current_setting('app.debug') = 'true';  -- example pattern\n  IF debug_enabled THEN\n      SELECT 'p_id=' || p_id;\n  END IF;\n  ```\n\n- **`$ERROR ... $END` (compile-time error directive)** — drop the directive; the migrated code either compiles or doesn't and there is no compile-time guard.\n- **`$$identifier` definitions** (set via `ALTER SESSION SET PLSQL_CCFLAGS = 'name:value'`) — drop entirely; replace with constants in the procedure body or columns in a config table.\n- **`DBMS_PREPROCESSOR.PRINT_POST_PROCESSED_CODE`** — drop; no equivalent.\n\nConditional compilation that selects between **two functionally equivalent code paths** (different idioms for the same outcome) usually has only one branch worth migrating — pick the modern one and discard the legacy fallback. Conditional compilation that selects between **functionally different behaviors** (debug vs. prod, feature-flag-driven) must become a runtime check.\n\n### `DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE` and Oracle session state\n\n`DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE`, `DBMS_LOCK.SLEEP`, `DBMS_APPLICATION_INFO.SET_*`, and similar SQL\\*Plus / session-state procedures have no DBSQL equivalent inside a SQL procedure body. See `04-procedures-variables.md → DBMS_OUTPUT` for the full options (drop, replace with a `SELECT`, or move to a notebook log statement).\n\n## Construct quick-reference\n\nSee `references/oracle/00-quirks.md → Construct Routing Table` (eagerly loaded) for the full scan table mapping each PL/SQL construct to its reference file.\n"}}]);
